docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:56:## 2 How to read this document
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:67:2.3.e **Strength flags A and K.** Some floor items cannot fully hold against a process on the same host until two things exist: **A**, one operating-system account per agent (R-82); **K**, the operator key with its confirmation provider (R-76). A floor item that needs a flag holds in a weaker form until the flag exists, and the status call says which flags are absent (3.1.e). The weaker form is what TM already accepted as RR-2 (host root, always), RR-16 and RR-18; this step only says for how long (RA-1, RA-4).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:71:## 3 The security floor (card 3.1)
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:78:3.1.e **Status honesty.** The status call of R-6.e MUST name each strength flag that is absent ("no per-agent accounts", "no operator key", "no confirmation provider") and MUST NOT claim a defence against an adversary the present strength does not stop. This restates R-76.e and R-82.e as one floor rule; it is cheap and it is the control against ADV-3 applied to the floor itself.
