docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:364:4.3 Counts, over the 82 requirements, counted from the table by a script (the row class decides): **Floor** (alone, "Floor + Must" or "Floor in part"): 35. **Must**: 34 (R-57 also has a Should part). **Should**: 6. **Could**: 1. **Deferred**: 3 (R-9, R-38, R-41), plus the operator leg of R-1, which waits for GP-11. **Excluded**: 2 (R-40, R-43). **Committed target**: 1 (R-82). 35 + 34 + 6 + 1 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 82.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:368:## 5 The basic version (card 3.3)
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:419:## 6 Phases (card 3.4)
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:444:6.2.4.a Scope. (1) The directory and project identity (R-60); the roster, cards signed with the hub signing key that PH-2 introduced (R-61) with a sequence number and time-to-live, home-hub binding as R-60.a (2e) rules it, the project root key, signed key rotation and "moved to", the no-guess rule, the optional per-hub list of project ids, the registration cap (R-77). (2) Tier 0 events and the route registry (R-72, 10.2): who asks, what, evidence, digest; one audit trail; a human-route event expires visibly after 10 minutes (PN-15); approvals digest-bound and single-use (SI-13); the built-in route and preconditions of each class (10.3.4.c) as a fixed table, with no configurable rule yet (NK; the rule engine arrives in PH-4); the confirmation-provider contract with a **stub provider for tests** and the interim controls of 10.4 (R-76.a (6)). (3) Every client verifies the declared hub id (R-54, FX-2 complete). (4) DEAD is signed and sequenced (R-60.a, R-65.a). (5) Versions on the card, the NTP check, the skew flag, cross-host refusal of an incompatible version (R-57 second part). (6) T-3347, the cross-hub read of a message's stage and reply by id, so a sender on one hub reads the record on another. (7) Cross-project and cross-hub conversations with admitted peers over the hub path. (8) Principle B′, stage 1: pin hub card-signing keys out of band (R-80). (9) The TOFU repair paths gated by a Tier 0 event (BP-19, PR-14). (10) The end of the `sidecar:` alias (R-71). (11) Peers see only reachable yes or no and the version class (R-53, CR-11). (12) Track A, stage A0, moved here from PH-2 in v0.2: the pilot account is created by a Tier 0 event of class C-15 (one event, one digest, human route, the interim approval of IC-2), so the machinery that R-82.a (3) and R-82.e require before an account exists is already live (6.2.4.a (2)).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:446:6.2.4.c Exit. (1) A forged card, a replayed old card, a restored roster, an unsigned hub-key change and a competing project claim are each refused or flagged, and the original binding still resolves (fixtures, then a two-hub live test with a negative control). (2) A Tier 0 event is decided on each route that has a subject in PH-3: the policy route for C-2a (a hub-key rotation signed by the old key) and C-6a (an agent-key replacement signed by the old key), by the built-in route of 10.3.4.c and recorded as `policy:builtin:<verb>`, while a hub-key change not signed by the old key (C-2b) is refused the policy route and goes to a human; the route of C-10 (a dead holder's hand-over) has no subject before PH-4, because it needs the R-67 lease successor and the hand-over, so in PH-3 it is exercised **by fixture only** and is tested live at the exit of PH-4 (6.2.5.c (9)); a human-route event unanswered for 10 minutes expires visibly and nothing takes effect; an approval whose digest differs, or whose executor or target differs, is refused; a replayed approval is refused; with no provider, the interim double approval is required and the status shows "no confirmation provider"; with the stub provider unreachable, a high-impact approval fails closed. (3) A mixed-version pair is refused loudly; a sender host 10 minutes fast makes no deadline judged from its timestamps. (4) A cross-hub message from an admitted peer reaches HANDED_OVER with the same five rows as a local one. (5) H-2, H-12, H-21, H-22, H-23 and H-28 are measured; the pilot account of A0 was created through its own Tier 0 event, and the plan with the measured price waits at the price gate for the operator (10.1.7). (6) T-2470, the charter rewording, is approved by the operator (6.6).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:462:6.2.7.a Scope. (1) Principle B′, stage 3: project mail and the custody key with its release rules; break-glass once K exists (R-80). (2) Track A, stage A4: the orchestrator no longer runs as `root` (10.1). (3) Track K: the real confirmation provider connected (T-3385 outcome); GP-11, the operator as a party to a conversation (R-1.e (3)). (4) R-36 digests; R-9 the startup chain, now that grants exist; R-41 typed work messages (planner); R-55 the send-and-wait tool (AEF). (5) Retire the shell sidecars. (6) Arc closing: every dimension of R-69 has run (section 8); every tripwire reviewed; the bypass inventory re-run; `scripts/arc-live-probe.sh` passes on every hub that needs the capability, so "shipped" means "live" (G-069).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:466:6.2.7.c.2 *Orchestrator without `root` (item 2, A4).* Each component returns to its previous service account by the one-command return of A2, one component at a time. The return is itself a Tier 0 act and the status call says "orchestrator runs as root" again (3.1.e). Accounts already created are kept; the helper stays fixed-purpose.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:486:6.5.2 **Track A, per-agent accounts.** Stages A0 to A4 are in 10.1. A0 starts in PH-3, once the Tier 0 route of C-15 is live (an account creation is a Tier 0 act, so it cannot precede that machinery); the read-only inventory of A0 may start earlier. A3 is recommended before PH-4 exits; A4 is in PH-6.
