docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:620:## 9 Hypotheses and their measurements (card 3.5)
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:656:| H-28 | Track A, stage A0 (PH-3) | An agent can do its ordinary work under its own account | One pilot agent runs a normal task cycle: read and edit in the shared checkout, commit with `fw git commit`, run tests, use TermLink, send and receive through the sidecar | At most 5 distinct permission fixes and no use of `root` | More than 15 distinct failures: the group-shared checkout of 10.1 needs another design before the migration is priced |
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:662:## 10 The items the rulings left to this step
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:664:10.1 **Per-agent operating-system accounts: order, price, group access to shared checkouts (R-82, OQ-2).**
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:665:10.1.1 What is ruled. The shared user is accepted for now. The committed target is one isolated account per agent, created by the orchestrator through one narrow privileged helper with fixed settings (never general root for the orchestrator); every creation is a Tier 0 act, human-approved and logged. Host root stays a residual for the lifetime of the design (R-82.a, RR-2). Step 3 phases and prices the move, including group-based access to shared checkouts. Step 4 designs the helper.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:666:10.1.2 What I found on the host on 2026-10-11 (1.4.d). 86 process lines that mention `claude` run as `root`, 16 as the operator's own login. `/opt/termlink` and its `.git`, `.tasks` and `.context` are `root:root` mode 775 with no shared group and `core.sharedRepository` unset. `useradd`, `sudo` and `setfacl` exist. Besides `root` there are two login accounts (the operator's, and `testdev`). The orchestrator is itself `root` **[H]**, so "never general root for the orchestrator" means changing the orchestrator too (stage A4).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:680:10.1.5 The price, by part. Sizes are my estimates **[A]** (S a day or two, M a week, L several weeks); the pilot (A0, H-28) replaces them with measurements.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:685:| Account template and helper | One template (home mode 0700, no login shell, no `sudo`, a group), one helper with fixed settings behind a single `sudoers` entry, one Tier 0 event per account created (R-82.a (3)) | M | A helper that does more than its fixed action becomes general root (R-82.d tests this) |
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:691:| Orchestrator and framework | The orchestrator, `runme.sh`, cron installation and hub restarts without general root (stage A4) | L | The project's operational scripts assume root |
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:693:| Verification | The tests of R-82.d: a helper asked for anything else refuses; no agent runs as `root`; agent A cannot read agent B's key; every creation has a Tier 0 event | M | |
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:700:10.1.7 The stages. A0 pilot: an inventory, then one non-critical agent under its own account, running a normal task cycle; the output is H-28 and a list of what needed root. **A price gate follows: the operator confirms the plan with the measured price.** A1 build the template, the group model, the helper, and the installed code; launcher starts sessions under accounts. A2 migrate in waves, the orchestrator last; one-command return per agent. A3 every agent on the host migrated; `ps` shows none as `root`; T-20 passes; the status call says "per-agent accounts: yes, host root: still a residual". A4 the orchestrator and the framework's scripts no longer need general root.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:702:10.1.8.a **A** Accounts first: A3 before the first operational release (PH-2). The biggest unknown (a re-platforming of how every agent starts) then blocks a demonstration that does not need it.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:703:10.1.8.b **B** Pilot early with a price gate; A3 before PH-4 exits; A4 in PH-6. PH-2 and PH-3 ship at the base strength of 3.7 with RA-1 stating it; the claim against same-host attackers is made only when A3 is done.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:706:10.1.8.e Recommendation **B**. Reason: until PH-4 the controls protect against honest failure, outages, drift and remote attackers, and they are worth having at the base strength. At PH-4 the system starts to hold configurable authority (grants, allow-lists, eligibility, membership), which is exactly the class of file "an agent cannot write" (SI-20, SI-21); from there the base strength is not enough. Starting the pilot in PH-3, as soon as the Tier 0 route that an account creation needs is live, prices the move with data before PH-4, and the gate lets the operator stop before committing. (v0.1 put the pilot in PH-2, ahead of that route; an account creation without its event would have broken R-82.a (3).) A is rejected because it makes the largest unknown the first thing built. C is rejected because it leaves the hijack-to-root path open through every phase that adds capability. D delays a target the operator has committed to.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:732:| C-15 | Create an agent operating-system account (R-82) | human | no | A Tier 0 act, ruled: **every** account creation is human-approved and logged, one event and one digest per account (R-82.a (3)). (v0.1 proposed one event per migration wave; that relaxed "every" and is withdrawn. A batch form would need a ruling change, see OP-1.) |
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:903:| IC-4 | Per-agent accounts | The status call, and the text of every floor claim | States "per-agent accounts: no" and what that means (3.7); no claim against ADV-2a | It prevents nothing | Stage A3 |
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:921:## 11 Residual risks for the operator, and the risk-acceptance requests
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:925:11.3 The requests, RA-1 to RA-9. Each is taken as its own question (13).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:926:11.3.1 **RA-1 The base strength against same-host adversaries lasts until accounts are done.**
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:927:11.3.1.a Deferred: the part of SI-3, SI-14, SI-15 (F-7), SI-17, SI-20, SI-21, SI-22 and FX-5 (custody) that stops a process on the same host. PH-1 to PH-4 and until stage A3.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:929:11.3.1.c Accepting means: until the agents have their own accounts, a hijacked agent on this host can read a sibling's keys and stores and sign as it, and a successful prompt injection (RR-1, accepted) ends in a root shell. This gives RR-2 ("accepted for now") an end: stage A3, which I recommend before PH-4 exits. If A3 slips past that, PH-4 ships at base strength and this request is asked again.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:975:## 12 Change requests to earlier steps
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:978:12.2 **CX-1** (step 2, threat model). Add a bypass route **BP-20**: "a shared writable checkout (and the harness's hook configuration inside it) as a lateral path between agent accounts" and the residual risk **RR-21** of RA-7. Consider an invariant **SI-32** [P]: the code that implements the floor, and the hook wiring, are not writable by any agent account and are verified against their installed hashes at session start (conditional on stage A1). Why: the route appears only once accounts exist (R-82) and no entry names it. Evidence: 10.1.4, 11.3.7.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:981:12.5 **CX-4** (step 1, requirements), bookkeeping, no requirement changes. Close the open items that point at this step once the operator rules: R-6.e (2) and R-70.g (the diagnosis-time bound and the state cap: section 7); R-45.a (reconciliation: 10.5); R-72.g and R-76.g (the initial classes: 10.2); R-73.g (the rule format: 10.3); R-82.g (phasing and price: 10.1); R-6.g (applying the rule to AEF's receiver: 7.4); and R-3.e (3), whose open bound can be set to the R-44.e "not handed over" deadline, the only number the rulings give (H-14 uses it).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:983:## 13 Open questions and decisions only the operator can take
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:985:13.1 The standing instruction (2026-10-01, profile P1.2.b) applies: **one question at a time**, each with its background, its options and a recommendation, and the next only after the operator says "next". The list below is the order I recommend: 23 questions. OP-1 to OP-5 are the items the hand-over named (8a, 8b twice, 8c, 8d). 055 is waiting for OP-4. OP-13 to OP-20 are the risk-acceptance requests RA-1 to RA-8 of section 11, each its own question. OP-21 to OP-23 were added in v0.2 after review (the hook delivery point, who writes the receive side, and RA-9); take them after OP-20, or earlier if the operator wishes, since OP-21 changes the entry phase of SI-18 and SI-23. Each question below is a header: the orchestrator turns it into a brief with `/decision-brief`; the full options and reasons are in the section named.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:987:13.2.1 **OP-1 Accounts: the order and the price (10.1.8).** **Ruled 2026-10-11: B with two amendments** (T-3352 Decisions): the read-only root inventory is a PH-0 exit item, and after the price gate no new agent is started as root. When does each agent get its own operating-system account, and when is the price decided? A, accounts complete before the first operational release. B, a pilot early with a price gate; A3 before PH-4 exits; A4 in PH-6. C, after PH-5. D, a pilot only; the migration becomes its own arc. Recommendation **B**. Settles: the end of RA-1, the shape of Track A. Note for this question: the ruling says every account creation is a Tier 0 act (R-82.a (3)), so the pilot and every later account is one event with its own digest; an event per migration wave (a batch digest) was in v0.1 and is withdrawn, and offering it would need a change to the ruling.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:999:13.2.13 **OP-13 to OP-20** **Ruled 2026-10-11: each as recommended in section 11** (confirmation list, T-3352 Decisions): are RA-1 to RA-8 in that order (RA-9 is OP-23, 13.2.16). For each: accept as written, accept with a change, or send back for a mitigation. Recommendation for each is in section 11 (RA-1 accept; RA-2 accept; RA-3 accept; RA-4 accept with a review point at the exit of PH-4; RA-5 accept; RA-6 accept; RA-7 accept as RR-21; RA-8 accept).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1005:## 14 Handoff: what goes to steps 4 to 8
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1009:14.1.b Open items the earlier steps and this step hand to step 4: the composition of the BV (5.1); where policy records live, how a sidecar fetches them and the stale rule (10.3.11); the hook wiring and the installed-code boundary (10.1.6, RA-7, CX-1); the design of the narrow privileged helper and the account template (R-82, 10.1.5); hub-side visibility of an inbox's consumers (SI-16, FP-1 failure 2); the 30-second component heartbeats (FP-1); the claim mechanism and the "transcript complete" signal per harness (R-51.a, R-47.a); the hub id minting and the mail-hub declaration format (R-61, R-54); grant fields (they are the `limits` of 10.3.4); the lease durations, quiet period and cooldown of "main" (R-67); the one-receiver observation window, the detection time for a second live copy of a project and the canary's staleness threshold (REQ 13.4.3); the skew bound, the gap-wait bound, the jitter, the adaptive cap and the hop limit (R-57, R-59, R-56); retention ceilings (R-35); roster format and enrolment (R-77); key pinning and rotation (R-75); the cost of re-wrapping message keys for long histories (R-74, R-80); the confirmation-provider interface after T-3385 (10.3.10); GP-11.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1012:14.2 To step 5 (evidence specialist). The staging table (section 8); the 17 fail-closed rows with their kill-checked controls (5.6); the six standing controls (6.2.1.a); the 28 hypotheses with their measurement designs (section 9); the five GP-12 fault injections (7.2.d); the reconciliation fixtures (10.5, T-18); the account tests (T-20, R-82.d). The test estate and agent pairs stay open for you (REQ 13.1).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1015:14.5 To step 8 (planner). Horizon changes and slices: T-3383 to `now` (6.2.1.a); one set of tasks per phase with the exit criteria of section 6 as acceptance criteria; the waves W1 to W4 of PH-2; Track A stages A0 to A4 as their own tasks with the price gate after A0; Track K as T-3385's outcome; the gates of 6.6; the dependency T-3387 before wave W2 and T-3386 before FX-1 is switched to "disable". "No knob before its lock" is a planning rule: a task that adds a configuration surface for authority must contain its lock.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1018:## 15 Coverage: the card, the task and the hand-over
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1024:| 6.1 Every SI-n and every irreversible-action requirement is in the floor or has a recorded risk-acceptance request | Section 3: 3.3 (SI-1 to SI-31), 3.4 (FX-1 to FX-8), 3.5 (all 33 security-typed requirements, each in the floor or listed), 3.6 (what is outside and why); section 11 (RA-1 to RA-9; RA-9 added in v0.2 for SI-7 on the existing rails) | Met. The floor is 39 items |
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1056:15.4 What REQ 13.1 and 13.5.1 handed to this step, beyond the ten: the ordering of the P1 and P2 items (4.2, section 6); the reconciliation details of the outage log (10.5); the phasing of R-72 to R-82 against the existing P1 items (4.2); the durability floor of the store (SI-15 in 3.3, FC-2 and FC-3 in 5.6, T-7 in section 8); the defence against ADV-1 to ADV-14 (3.7); the GP-12 application to AEF's receiver (7.4).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-03-floor-and-phasing.md:1060:15.5.3 The host readings (1.4.d) are one moment. They do not replace the count of agents in R-82.a.
