docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1018:6.17.1 Every requirement below comes from a change request of the threat model (step 2, T-3351, section 21) that the operator accepted, and from the operator's rulings recorded in the rulings register (22.18) and in the decision record of the T-3351 task file. Each statement is [R] unless tagged; [R~] marks a number or detail that the ruling accepted from the orchestrator and that may be changed with evidence; [P] marks a clause that no ruling states. Priorities are collector proposals (6.1.c). "Open" lists what the ruling leaves to a later step. R-82 (added in v0.5.1) comes from a step-2 ruling that is not a change request (OQ-2); it is listed in 14.1 beside the change requests.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1029:R-72.g Status: not built; the re-pin record is T-3384. Until per-agent accounts (OQ-2, R-82) and an operator key (R-76) exist, the routes run on hosts where agents are root, so the event is detection and discipline, not a lock (RR-2, RR-18). Open: the list of low-risk classes (step 3 or 4); the charter rewording the card exchange needs (T-2470, 13.2.2).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1077:R-78.g Status: not built. Depends on R-76 (and, against same-user processes, on R-82): without an operator key, a file the agent "cannot write" does not exist on a host with one operating-system user. It does not stop host root, with or without an operator key (RR-2, RR-9 and RR-12, accepted).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1103:R-82 **Per-agent operating-system accounts: the committed target.**
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1104:R-82.a [R] Ruled (OQ-2 C now, B as the committed target, 2026-10-07; added to this document in v0.5.1; operator: "we accept it for now, but we definitely want to move to a more isolated per-agent account, which can be created by the orchestrator ... always via Tier 0"): (1) Now: the shared operating-system user is accepted (RR-2 accepted). On .107 all 19 Claude agents run as root (ps, 2026-10-07), so the shared user is root and same-user equals host root in practice. (2) Target: one isolated operating-system account per agent, created by the orchestrator through a narrow privileged helper: one helper with fixed settings (for example a single sudoers entry), never general root for the orchestrator. (3) Every account creation is a Tier 0 act: human-approved and logged (R-72). (4) Step 3 phases and prices the move, including group-based access to shared checkouts; step 4 designs the helper. (5) Host root stays a permanent residual for the lifetime of the system (RR-2): account separation does not remove it.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1105:R-82.b Security invariant, operational · P1 [P] (the ruling commits the target and fixes no phase; the priority is a collector proposal) · source OQ-2 (T-3351 decision 2026-10-07).
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1106:R-82.c Rationale: one account per workload is the standard pattern (service users, systemd DynamicUser, Kubernetes service accounts); it makes per-agent keys (R-75) and operator-signed files (R-78) a real boundary, and it ends agents running as root. Until then a malicious process of the same user reads a sibling's key.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1107:R-82.d Verification: per phase of step 3: a fixture where the helper is asked for anything but its fixed action and refuses; a check that no agent process runs as root; a fixture where agent A's process reads agent B's key file and fails; an audit-trail check that each creation has a Tier 0 event.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1108:R-82.e Acceptance. Given a request to create an agent account, then a Tier 0 event is raised and approved before the helper acts, and the audit trail holds the decision. Given the helper receives a request outside its fixed settings, then it refuses. Given per-agent accounts exist, then one agent cannot read another agent's key files; host root still can, and the status says so rather than claiming otherwise.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1109:R-82.f Holds against: ADV-2, ADV-7 (the same-user cases); not against host root.
docs/design/interactive-agent-communication-01-requirements.md:1110:R-82.g Status: not built; accepted for now. Open: phasing, price and group-based access to shared checkouts (step 3); helper design (step 4). R-72.g, R-78.g and R-80.g state their residuals "until per-agent accounts exist" and depend on this requirement.
